{
  "account": "plur",
  "background": {"color": "#0A0A0A"},
  "slides": [
    {
      "type": "cover",
      "main": "Six Seconds Changed the World.",
      "sub": "Jungle — East London, 1991"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "text": [
        "1969.",
        "A drummer named Gregory C. Coleman",
        "played a six-second solo.",
        "That recording became the Amen Break."
      ],
      "sub": "It never stopped looping."
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "text": [
        "1990. The acid house heat faded.",
        "Breakbeat accelerated.",
        "Bass got heavier.",
        "Dancehall vocals slipped in."
      ],
      "sub": "They called it Breakbeat Hardcore."
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "text": [
        "1991. East London. Forest Gate.",
        "'We Are I.E.' — Lennie De Ice.",
        "Amen Break, reggae bass, North African melody.",
        "No one had heard this before."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "text": [
        "1992. The scene split.",
        "Happy or Dark.",
        "Pirate radio Kool FM 94.5 chose Dark.",
        "The music still had no name."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "quote",
      "quote": [
        "Jungle Techno.",
        "Hardcore Jungle."
      ],
      "note": "They were searching for the word. They called it Jungle."
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "text": [
        "Fabio. Grooverider. DJ Hype. DJ Ron.",
        "AWOL. Telepathy. Roast.",
        "160 BPM shook the floors.",
        "Only they knew this music."
      ],
      "sub": "Then came Drum and Bass. Grime. Dubstep. All from here."
    },
    {
      "type": "cta",
      "text": [
        "A six-second drum solo.",
        "Looping for thirty years."
      ],
      "strobe": "It started in a basement. It never ended.",
      "cta": "Save this. The loop goes on."
    }
  ]
}
